<td id="S3.T2.4.1.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0.302\pm 0.013}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.302</mn><mo>±</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0.302\pm 0.013}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.4.1.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.4.1.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">CBEC</th>
<td id="S3.T2.4.1.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.416\pm 0.020" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.416</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.020</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.416\pm 0.020</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.4.1.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.335\pm 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.335</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.335\pm 0.014</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Main results. ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Selective Prediction for Diabetic Retinopathy ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualises these results: <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates across the full coverage range (left), with non-overlapping bootstrap interquartile ranges against scalar baselines (right).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/paper_selective_prediction_main_bootstrap_bnn.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Selective prediction for DR.
<span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Critical FNR vs. coverage;
<math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates all baselines across the full range.
<span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Bootstrap AUSC distribution (<math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>);
<math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-based policies (red/orange) show non-overlapping interquartile ranges against scalar baselines (grey).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The improvement over raw variance is especially striking.
Sale_EU_crit and Var_crit (AUSC <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.650" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.650</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.650</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.606" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.606</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.606</annotation></semantics></math>) perform <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">worse than entropy</em> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.604" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.604</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.604</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that boundary suppression (Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Variance Bound on the Simplex). ‣ 2.3 Why Variance Alone Fails: Boundary Suppression ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) severely confounds unnormalised variance for critical classes with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\approx 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\approx 0.06</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> normalisation in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> corrects precisely this: Grade 3 has mean probability <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.06, giving entropy curvature <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>17, so even small variance there carries large information-theoretic weight. This normalisation benefit is robust to the choice of inference method:
a deep ensemble <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakshminarayanan et al., 2017</a>]</cite> of five members yields <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> AUSC <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.390" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.390</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.390</annotation></semantics></math>
vs. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.447" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.447</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.447</annotation></semantics></math> for Sale_EU_crit (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduction; Appendix <a href="#A4.SS12" title="D.12 Deep Ensemble Validation: Per-Class Metrics ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretability: Error Signatures.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A distinctive advantage of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> over scalar MI is that it reveals the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structure</em> of model confusion.
Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Interpretability: Error Signatures. ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Selective Prediction for Diabetic Retinopathy ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that catastrophic misses (Grade 3 predicted as Grade 0) and severity underestimates (Grade 3 predicted as Grade 2) have nearly identical scalar MI (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.024</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.027</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.027</annotation></semantics></math> nats) but very different <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> signatures.
The catastrophic miss concentrates epistemic mass on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, identifying Grade 2 as the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bottleneck</em> of confusion, while the severity underestimate elevates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating doubt about healthy status.
These qualitatively different failure modes, invisible to scalar metrics, suggest
distinct remediation strategies; the epistemic confusion matrix
(Appendix <a href="#A4.SS10" title="D.10 Epistemic Confusion Matrix ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.10</span></a>) further confirms that cross-boundary confusion
is <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7\times</annotation></semantics></math> stronger than within-group confusion, validating the clinical
partition (detailed analysis in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS9" title="D.9 Error Signatures ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/diagnostic_error_signatures.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Epistemic signatures for Grade 3 errors with similar MI but distinct <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> patterns.
<span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Catastrophic miss (<math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, MI<math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math>0.024) concentrates on <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Centre:</span> Severity underestimate (<math id="S3.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>, MI<math id="S3.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math>0.027) elevates <math id="S3.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Grouped comparison showing the <math id="S3.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> fingerprints differ markedly despite similar MI, the catastrophic miss (red) peaks at <math id="S3.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> while the severity error (dark red) peaks at <math id="S3.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MC dropout comparison.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test robustness to the posterior approximation, we repeat the evaluation using
standard MC dropout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gal and Ghahramani, 2016</a>]</cite> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S{=}30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S{=}30</annotation></semantics></math>).
The ranking changes markedly: CBEC achieves the lowest AUSC (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.197\pm 0.012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.197</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.197\pm 0.012</annotation></semantics></math>,
winning 100% of bootstrap samples), a 53.6% reduction over MI (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.425\pm 0.024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.425</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.425\pm 0.024</annotation></semantics></math>),
while <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> drops to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.419" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.419</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.419</annotation></semantics></math>.
MC dropout is known to produce regions of spurious confidence where the approximate
posterior underestimates uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith and Gal, 2018</a>]</cite>. In our
framework, this inflates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for critical classes, degrading the Taylor
approximation underlying <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> while leaving CBEC’s correlation gate intact.
This confirms the intended complementarity: <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the primary metric under well-calibrated posteriors; CBEC provides a robust
fallback when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> signals degradation.</em>
Full results in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS11" title="D.11 Full Bootstrap Results: MC Dropout ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Out-of-Distribution Detection</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the per-class epistemic uncertainty metric on out-of-distribution (OoD) detection, where elevated epistemic uncertainty should signal distributional shift.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two OoD detection tasks spanning different modalities and class structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FashionMNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2017</a>]</cite> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> KMNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clanuwat et al., 2018</a>]</cite>:</span> A 10-class image classification task where the model is trained on FashionMNIST (50,000 train, 10,000 test) and evaluated on KMNIST (10,000 samples) as OoD.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MIMIC-III ICU</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al., 2016</a>]</cite> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Newborn:</span> A binary mortality prediction task on tabular clinical data (44 features) where the model is trained on adult ICU admissions (40,406 train, 4,490 test) and evaluated on the Newborn unit (5,357 samples) as OoD.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both tasks use low-rank Bayesian neural networks with Gaussian posteriors.
FashionMNIST: two hidden layers (1200 units, rank 25), <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S{=}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S{=}50</annotation></semantics></math> MC samples.
MIMIC-III: two hidden layers (128 units, rank 15), <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S{=}512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S{=}512</annotation></semantics></math> MC samples, with class-weighted training to address mortality imbalance (8.6% prevalence). See Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Model Architecture and Training ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a> for full architectural and training details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare four uncertainty scores: negative maximum softmax probability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2017</a>]</cite>, MI, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}=\sum_{k}\sigma^{2}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}=\sum_{k}\sigma^{2}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and our proposed <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Performance is measured by AUROC(ID samples labelled 0, OoD samples labelled 1); results are mean <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std over 5 seeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Out-of-Distribution Detection ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarises OoD detection performance.
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the highest AUROC on both datasets: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.735\pm 0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.735</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.735\pm 0.009</annotation></semantics></math> on FashionMNIST versus <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.724" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.724</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.724</annotation></semantics></math> for MI, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.815\pm 0.017" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.815</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.017</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.815\pm 0.017</annotation></semantics></math> on MIMIC-III versus <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.802" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.802</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.802</annotation></semantics></math> for MI.
The OoD-to-ID ratio column reveals the mechanism: on FashionMNIST, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> amplifies the distributional shift signal to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="6.43\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.43</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.43\times</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.92\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.92</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.92\times</annotation></semantics></math> for MI.
On FashionMNIST, this effect is predicted by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>: the gap <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}-\text{MI}\approx\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}m_{3,k}/\mu_{k}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext>MI</mtext></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}-\text{MI}\approx\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}m_{3,k}/\mu_{k}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> grows with posterior skewness, and OoD samples produce higher skewness diagnostic <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> than ID samples across all ten classes (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS6" title="E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ FashionMNIST (𝐾=10). ‣ E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
On MIMIC-III the picture is more nuanced: the survival class (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>) follows the expected pattern (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}^{\text{OOD}}&gt;\rho_{0}^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>OOD</mtext></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>ID</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}^{\text{OOD}}&gt;\rho_{0}^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math>, median shift <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+43\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>43</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+43\%</annotation></semantics></math>), but the mortality class (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>) reverses because near-zero <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for newborns collapses <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> faster than <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can compensate, concentrating the third-order signal entirely in class 0 (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS6" title="E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The unnormalised baseline <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> underperforms MI on both datasets
despite matching its ratio on FashionMNIST (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.92\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.92</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.92\times</annotation></semantics></math>): without
mean-normalisation, raw variance <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded above by
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}(1-\mu_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}(1-\mu_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, compressing the dynamic range of uncertainty scores.
Strikingly, on MIMIC-III <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">highest</em> ratio
(<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.71\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.71</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71\times</annotation></semantics></math>) yet the <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lowest</em> AUROC among epistemic measures.
A high mean ratio does not imply good separability: AUROC depends on the
full distributional overlap, and the un-normalised variance sum concentrates
both ID and OoD scores near zero with a shared heavy tail, so the
numerically large mean shift is swamped by within-group spread
(Appendix <a href="#A5.SS5" title='E.5 Score Distribution Analysis and the 𝐸⁢𝑈_"var" Paradox ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.5</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A5.F12" title='Figure 12 ‣ E.5 Score Distribution Analysis and the 𝐸⁢𝑈_"var" Paradox ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). This pattern is consistent across inference methods: a deep ensemble
replicates the same ranking (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}{=}0.753&gt;\text{MI}{=}0.752&gt;EU_{\text{var}}{=}0.750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.753</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mtext>MI</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.752</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}{=}0.753&gt;\text{MI}{=}0.752&gt;EU_{\text{var}}{=}0.750</annotation></semantics></math>) on MIMIC-III, with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math>
again achieving the highest ratio (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.72\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.72\times</annotation></semantics></math>) and the lowest AUROC (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 Deep Ensemble OoD Detection: MIMIC-III ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
