</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Complete Metric Reference</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix collects, in self-contained form, the definitions and
interpretations of every uncertainty metric evaluated in the selective
prediction experiments.
Throughout, we adopt the notation of Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:
<math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> stochastic forward passes produce probability vectors
<math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}(x)\in\Delta^{K-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}(x)\in\Delta^{K-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1,\dots,S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1,\dots,S</annotation></semantics></math>, from which all
subsequent quantities are estimated.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Monte Carlo Prediction Statistics</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following sample statistics, computed from the <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> stochastic
forward passes, serve as building blocks for all metrics defined
below.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mean prediction.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The predictive mean</p>
<table id="A3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{k}\;=\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}p_{k}^{(s)},\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\;=\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}p_{k}^{(s)},\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">represents the model’s consensus probability for class <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The predicted label is <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\arg\max_{k}\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\arg\max_{k}\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-class variance and covariance.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sample variance and covariance, with Bessel correction, are</p>
<table id="A3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{k}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{k}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{S-1}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k}\bigr)^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{S-1}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k}\bigr)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Sigma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Sigma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{S-1}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{i}^{(s)}-\mu_{i}\bigr)\bigl(p_{j}^{(s)}-\mu_{j}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{S-1}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{i}^{(s)}-\mu_{i}\bigr)\bigl(p_{j}^{(s)}-\mu_{j}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with Pearson correlation
<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ij}=\Sigma_{ij}/(\sigma_{i}\,\sigma_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ij}=\Sigma_{ij}/(\sigma_{i}\,\sigma_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
A strongly negative <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that probability mass
flows systematically from class <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to class <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> across forward
passes—i.e. the model actively confuses the two classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Third central moment.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{3,k}\;=\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k}\bigr)^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{3,k}\;=\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\bigl(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k}\bigr)^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This quantity enters only through the skewness diagnostic <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined in Section <a href="#A3.SS7" title="C.7 Diagnostic Metrics ‣ Appendix C Complete Metric Reference ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Scalar Uncertainty Baselines</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following standard metrics assign a single uncertainty score to
each input and do not distinguish between classes.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Predictive entropy.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Shannon entropy of the mean prediction,</p>
<table id="A3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H[\bar{p}]\;=\;-\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\mu_{k}\log\mu_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.058em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[\bar{p}]\;=\;-\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\mu_{k}\log\mu_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">lies in <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\log K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\log K]</annotation></semantics></math> and captures <em id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total</em> predictive
uncertainty—both epistemic and aleatoric.
It cannot distinguish a model that lacks training data from one
facing a genuinely ambiguous input; nor does it differentiate
clinically benign confusions (e.g. Grade 0 vs. Grade 1) from
dangerous ones (Grade 0 vs. Grade 3).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mutual information.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MI}\;=\;H[\bar{p}]\;-\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}H\bigl[\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>MI</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MI}\;=\;H[\bar{p}]\;-\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}H\bigl[\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H[\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}]=-\sum_{k}p_{k}^{(s)}\log p_{k}^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}]=-\sum_{k}p_{k}^{(s)}\log p_{k}^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
MI isolates epistemic uncertainty by subtracting the average
per-pass entropy (the aleatoric component): if all <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> passes
produce identical softmax vectors, MI vanishes regardless of how
uncertain those vectors are.
While MI is the standard scalar epistemic measure, it remains a
single number that cannot reveal <em id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> classes drive the
model’s disagreement.
Two inputs with identical MI may involve entirely different
confusion patterns, as demonstrated by the error signatures in
Section <a href="#A4.SS9" title="D.9 Error Signatures ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Maximum softmax uncertainty.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MaxProb}\;=\;1-\max_{k}\;\mu_{k}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>MaxProb</mi><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MaxProb}\;=\;1-\max_{k}\;\mu_{k}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the simplest deferral baseline: it uses only the mean
prediction and does not exploit MC disagreement.
MaxProb is insensitive to the <em id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution</em> of probability
across non-top classes; two inputs
<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(0.6,\,0.4,\,0,\,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(0.6,\,0.4,\,0,\,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(0.6,\,0,\,0.4,\,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(0.6,\,0,\,0.4,\,0)</annotation></semantics></math> receive
identical scores despite the latter involving a dangerous
safe–critical confusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Per-Class Variance Baselines</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These metrics use the raw per-class variance <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
proxy for epistemic disagreement, without the <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
normalisation that defines <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critical variance.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var\_crit}\;=\;\max_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\;\sigma_{k}^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>crit</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var\_crit}\;=\;\max_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\;\sigma_{k}^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of critical
(treatment-requiring) classes.
This provides a targeted measure of MC disagreement restricted to
critical classes, but inherits the base-rate confound identified in
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Variance Bound on the Simplex). ‣ 2.3 Why Variance Alone Fails: Boundary Suppression ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>: a class with <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> naturally
exhibits higher variance than one with <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, even at equal
levels of epistemic disagreement in logit space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Global and critical variance baselines.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sale et al. [2024]</a></cite> explicitly define the global variance-based
epistemic uncertainty measure (their equation 22):</p>
<table id="A3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sale\_EU\_global}\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\sigma_{k}^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Sale</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EU</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>global</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sale\_EU\_global}\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\sigma_{k}^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which aggregates label-wise variance over all classes and constitutes
a scalar epistemic measure directly comparable to MI and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We additionally construct a critical-class restriction not defined by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sale et al. [2024]</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="A3.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sale\_EU\_crit}\;=\;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\sigma_{k}^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Sale</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>EU</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>crit</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sale\_EU\_crit}\;=\;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\sigma_{k}^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which provides a targeted analogue of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sum</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}</annotation></semantics></math> without
curvature normalisation.
Both variants suffer from the same boundary suppression as
<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var\_crit}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>crit</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var\_crit}</annotation></semantics></math>: for critical classes with mean prediction
<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\approx 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\approx 0.06</annotation></semantics></math> in our data, variance is bounded by
<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}(1-\mu_{k})\approx 0.056" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.056</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}(1-\mu_{k})\approx 0.056</annotation></semantics></math> regardless of the degree of model
disagreement (cf. Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Why Variance Alone Fails: Boundary Suppression ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>One-vs-All MI Baseline</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative per-class decomposition computes binary mutual
information for each class independently.
For class <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, define the binary variable
<math id="A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}^{(s)}=(p_{k}^{(s)},\;1-p_{k}^{(s)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}^{(s)}=(p_{k}^{(s)},\;1-p_{k}^{(s)})</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding
binary MI</p>
<table id="A3.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MI}_{k}^{\mathrm{bin}}\;=\;H[\bar{q}_{k}]\;-\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}H\bigl[q_{k}^{(s)}\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>MI</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>bin</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MI}_{k}^{\mathrm{bin}}\;=\;H[\bar{q}_{k}]\;-\;\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}H\bigl[q_{k}^{(s)}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The deferral score sums over critical classes:
<math id="A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{OvA\_MI}=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\mathrm{MI}_{k}^{\mathrm{bin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OvA</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>MI</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>MI</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>bin</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{OvA\_MI}=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\mathrm{MI}_{k}^{\mathrm{bin}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This measure is exact (not Taylor-approximated) but discards the
multi-class structure: it does not reveal which non-<math id="A3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> classes
compete with class <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the per-class binary MI terms are not
additive components of the full multiclass MI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Proposed Metrics: Per-Class Epistemic Decomposition</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The metrics in this subsection are derived from the second-order
Taylor expansion of mutual information developed in
Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-class epistemic contribution.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The central quantity is the per-class component</p>
<table id="A3.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}\;=\;\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}}{\mu_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}\;=\;\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\sigma_{k}^{2}}{\mu_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which satisfies <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}C_{k}\approx\mathrm{MI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>MI</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}C_{k}\approx\mathrm{MI}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 (MI Approximation). ‣ 2.1 From Scalar MI to a Per-Class Decomposition ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
The <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> weighting originates from the curvature of the
entropy surface (<math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{2}H/\partial p_{k}^{2}=-1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{2}H/\partial p_{k}^{2}=-1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>):
a class with <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> has curvature <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>, so even modest
probability variance there carries substantial
information-theoretic weight, whereas a class with <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>
requires proportionally larger variance to indicate genuine
epistemic uncertainty.
The ratio <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}^{2}/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}^{2}/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the classical
index of dispersion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cox and Lewis, 1966</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fano, 1947</a>]</cite>; the
<math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> prefactor arises from the Hessian structure,
ensuring that the components sum to MI rather than twice MI.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The approximation is reliable when the MC distribution for each
class is approximately symmetric.
Reliability is diagnosed by the skewness indicator <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Section <a href="#A3.SS7" title="C.7 Diagnostic Metrics ‣ Appendix C Complete Metric Reference ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.7</span></a>): when <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&gt;0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&gt;0.3</annotation></semantics></math> for a critical
class, <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> should be interpreted with caution and CBEC
(Section <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 Boundary-Aware Metrics ‣ Appendix C Complete Metric Reference ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>) is preferred.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critical-class aggregations.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two natural aggregations restrict attention to the critical class
set <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{\mathrm{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{\mathrm{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;=\;\max_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\;C_{k}\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;=\;\max_{k\in\mathcal{C}}\;C_{k}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sum</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;=\;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}C_{k}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;=\;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{C}}C_{k}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sensitive to single-class spikes: it
fires when <em id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> critical class carries elevated epistemic
uncertainty, and is the recommended aggregation when <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&lt;0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&lt;0.3</annotation></semantics></math>
for all critical classes.
<math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sum</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{crit\_sum}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smoother alternative that captures
cumulative uncertainty when confusion is distributed across
multiple critical classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.6 </span>Boundary-Aware Metrics</h3>

<div id="A3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The metrics above score each class (or class subset) in isolation.
In applications with a clinically meaningful partition into safe
classes <math id="A3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and critical classes
<math id="A3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the most dangerous errors are those that
cross this boundary.
The following metric targets such cross-boundary confusion directly.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-boundary epistemic confusion (CBEC).</h4>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBEC}(x)\;=\;\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}\;\sum_{j\in\mathcal{C}}\sqrt{C_{i}\cdot C_{j}}\;\cdot\;\max(0,\,-\rho_{ij})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>CBEC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBEC}(x)\;=\;\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}\;\sum_{j\in\mathcal{C}}\sqrt{C_{i}\cdot C_{j}}\;\cdot\;\max(0,\,-\rho_{ij}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBEC is non-zero only when three conditions hold simultaneously:
a safe class carries elevated <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, a critical class carries
elevated <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and their MC predictions are negatively correlated
(<math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ij}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ij}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that probability mass flows
systematically from one to the other across stochastic passes.
The geometric mean <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <em id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> classes to
be uncertain, and the correlation gate
<math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(0,-\rho_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(0,-\rho_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> filters coincidental co-elevation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CBEC is partially robust to degradation of the Taylor approximation
for two reasons.
First, the correlation <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed directly from MC
samples and does not depend on the second-order expansion.
Second, the geometric mean compresses outlier inflation: if
skewness inflates <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> by a factor of <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the geometric
mean is inflated by only <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These properties explain CBEC’s empirical robustness under MC
dropout, where <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates for rare critical classes degrade
(Section <a href="#A4.SS11" title="D.11 Full Bootstrap Results: MC Dropout ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.7 </span>Diagnostic Metrics</h3>

<div id="A3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following quantities assess the reliability and structure of the
<math id="A3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates.
They are not used as deferral scores.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Skewness diagnostic.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each class <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the ratio</p>
<table id="A3.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\;=\;\frac{|m_{3,k}|}{3\,\mu_{k}\cdot\sigma_{k}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\;=\;\frac{|m_{3,k}|}{3\,\mu_{k}\cdot\sigma_{k}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">equals the magnitude of the third-order Taylor correction relative
to the second-order term <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).
When <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the cubic remainder is negligible and <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
reliable; when <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&gt;0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&gt;0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, the second-order approximation is
degrading and <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> should be supplemented or replaced by CBEC.
In practice, majority classes (high <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, far from the simplex
boundary) produce approximately symmetric MC distributions and low
<math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; rare classes (<math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>) produce right-skewed
distributions bounded by zero and correspondingly higher <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Epistemic profiles.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each true class <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the normalised conditional expectation</p>
<table id="A3.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Profile}_{k}(i)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[\frac{C_{k}}{\textstyle\sum_{j}C_{j}}\;\Big|\;y=i\Bigr],\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Profile</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" lspace="0.280em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Profile}_{k}(i)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[\frac{C_{k}}{\textstyle\sum_{j}C_{j}}\;\Big|\;y=i\Bigr],\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">reveals the average distribution of epistemic uncertainty across
classes for inputs of true class <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, a profile <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.14,\,0.27,\,0.55,\,0.05)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.27</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.55</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.05</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.14,\,0.27,\,0.55,\,0.05)</annotation></semantics></math> for Grade 0
images indicates that the model’s uncertainty about healthy images
concentrates predominantly on Grade 2 (moderate DR), signalling
systematic Grade 0–Grade 2 confusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Error epistemic signatures.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conditioning on both true and predicted labels yields the
error-type signature</p>
<table id="A3.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Sig}_{k}(i\!\to\!j)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\bigl[C_{k}\;\big|\;y=i,\;\hat{y}=j\bigr],\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sig</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sig}_{k}(i\!\to\!j)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\bigl[C_{k}\;\big|\;y=i,\;\hat{y}=j\bigr],\qquad k=0,\dots,K{-}1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Different error types produce distinct <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> fingerprints even when
their scalar MI is similar, enabling targeted diagnostic
interpretation (see Section <a href="#A4.SS9" title="D.9 Error Signatures ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Epistemic confusion matrix.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[\sqrt{C_{i}\cdot C_{j}}\;\cdot\;\max(0,-\rho_{ij})\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[\sqrt{C_{i}\cdot C_{j}}\;\cdot\;\max(0,-\rho_{ij})\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Entry <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the average directed epistemic confusion
between classes <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, aggregated over the test set.
The matrix is symmetric with zero diagonal (since
<math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{kk}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{kk}=1</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(0,-\rho_{kk})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(0,-\rho_{kk})=0</annotation></semantics></math>).
High values in the <math id="A3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> block indicate
that the model’s epistemic uncertainty concentrates at the
clinically dangerous boundary, validating both the safe–critical
partition and the CBEC aggregation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.8 </span>Evaluation Metrics for Selective Prediction</h3>

<div id="A3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following metrics evaluate the quality of a deferral policy that,
at coverage level <math id="A3.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, retains a fraction <math id="A3.SS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of test inputs for
autonomous prediction and defers the remainder to human review.
Let <math id="A3.SS8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{kept}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{kept}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of retained samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critical false-negative rate.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Critical\;FNR}(c)\;=\;\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]\cdot\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}\notin\mathcal{C}]}{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Critical</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>FNR</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Critical\;FNR}(c)\;=\;\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]\cdot\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}\notin\mathcal{C}]}{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the primary clinical safety metric: among the critical-class
samples that the system decides autonomously (does not defer), it
measures the fraction misclassified as safe.
Crucially, within-critical errors (e.g. Grade 3 predicted as
Grade 2) are <em id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> counted, since the patient still receives
treatment; only boundary crossings into <math id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are penalised.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critical error rate.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Critical\;Err}(c)\;=\;\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]\cdot\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}\neq y_{n}]}{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Critical</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Err</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Critical\;Err}(c)\;=\;\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]\cdot\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}\neq y_{n}]}{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[y_{n}\in\mathcal{C}]}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A broader metric that includes within-critical misclassifications.
We report it alongside Critical FNR for completeness but consider
Critical FNR the primary safety metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Area under the selective risk curve (AUSC).</h4>

<div id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{AUSC}\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}R(c)\,dc\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>AUSC</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AUSC}\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}R(c)\,dc\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c)</annotation></semantics></math> is the risk (Critical FNR or error rate) at
coverage <math id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">El-Yaniv and Wiener, 2010</a>]</cite>.
AUSC provides a single-number summary that integrates risk over
all possible operating points, so a policy with low AUSC is
consistently safe regardless of the chosen coverage threshold.
We approximate the integral via the trapezoidal rule at
<math id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=200</annotation></semantics></math> equally spaced coverage levels; 95% confidence intervals
are obtained from 200 bootstrap resamples of the test set.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that AUSC and Critical FNR at a fixed operating point can
disagree: a policy that dominates at low coverages (50–70%) but
is inferior at 80% will produce favourable AUSC yet a suboptimal
fixed-point FNR.
This phenomenon appears in the MC dropout experiment
(Section <a href="#A4.SS11" title="D.11 Full Bootstrap Results: MC Dropout ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accuracy and macro F1.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard classification metrics computed on the retained set:</p>
<table id="A3.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Accuracy}(c)=\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}=y_{n}]}{|\mathrm{kept}(c)|},\qquad\mathrm{Macro\;F1}(c)=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}F_{1,k}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Accuracy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>kept</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Macro</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F1</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Accuracy}(c)=\frac{\sum_{n\in\mathrm{kept}(c)}\mathbb{1}[\hat{y}_{n}=y_{n}]}{|\mathrm{kept}(c)|},\qquad\mathrm{Macro\;F1}(c)=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}F_{1,k}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS8.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In our setting, accuracy is dominated by the majority class
(Grade 0 constitutes approximately 70% of the test set) and can be
misleading: a policy that defers all minority-class samples achieves
high accuracy but poor safety.
Macro F1 partially corrects the class-prevalence bias by weighting
classes equally, but remains an aggregate metric that does not
specifically target clinical safety.
We report both alongside the primary Critical FNR.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Dataset and Preprocessing</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We pool three public DR grading datasets: EyePACS (<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35{,}108" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35,108</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35{,}108</annotation></semantics></math> images), APTOS 2019 (<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{,}662" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,662</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{,}662</annotation></semantics></math>), and Messidor-2 (<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}200</annotation></semantics></math>), totalling <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39{,}970" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39,970</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39{,}970</annotation></semantics></math> fundus images.
The original five-level International Clinical DR Scale is consolidated into four grades: Grade 0 (no DR, 70.4%), Grade 1 (mild NPDR, 7.4%), Grade 2 (moderate NPDR, 16.3%), and Grade 3 (severe NPDR + PDR, 5.8%).
We merge the two highest grades because both require urgent referral, and the small severe NPDR subgroup is insufficient for reliable posterior estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Images are resized to <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> pixels.
Data are split patient-stratified into train/val/test = 28,018/4,004/7,948, with a patient-leakage check confirming no patient appears in multiple splits.
The test set preserves the class distribution: Grade 0 = 5,584 (70.3%), Grade 1 = 587 (7.4%), Grade 2 = 1,322 (16.6%), Grade 3 = 455 (5.7%).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Model Architecture and Training</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bayesian EfficientNet-B4.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a fully Bayesian variant of EfficientNet-B4 (width multiplier <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4</annotation></semantics></math>, depth multiplier <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.8</annotation></semantics></math>) using the low-rank variational framework of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toure and Stephens [2026]</a></cite>, which parameterises each weight matrix as <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=AB^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=AB^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with learned posteriors on the factors <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing the parameter count from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(mn)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(mn)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(r(m+n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(r(m+n))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The architecture comprises a stem convolution (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\to 48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\to 48</annotation></semantics></math> filters), 32 MBConv blocks across 7 stages, a top expansion (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="448\to 1792" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>448</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1792</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">448\to 1792</annotation></semantics></math>), and a classification head (Conv <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1792\to 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1792</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1792\to 256</annotation></semantics></math>, GAP, Dense <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\to 128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\to 128</annotation></semantics></math>, Dropout, Dense <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128\to 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128\to 4</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Pointwise (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math>) and expansion convolutions use low-rank factorisation <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=AB^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=AB^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with depth-aware rank selection: compression increases from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> at early layers to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="25\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\times</annotation></semantics></math> at deep layers, with rank <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\lfloor d_{\text{in}}d_{\text{out}}/(\text{compression}\cdot(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}))\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>compression</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\lfloor d_{\text{in}}d_{\text{out}}/(\text{compression}\cdot(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}))\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
Depthwise convolutions and squeeze-excitation <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> convolutions use direct (full-rank) variational parameterisation.
Dense layers in the classification head use the same low-rank factorisation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All variational layers use the local reparameterisation trick with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\mu+\mathrm{softplus}(\rho)\odot\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\mu+\mathrm{softplus}(\rho)\odot\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The prior is a scale-mixture Gaussian <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{1}^{2})+(1-\pi)\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{2}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{1}^{2})+(1-\pi)\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{2}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}=e^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}=e^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Posterior initialisation uses He-scaled uniform means with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> initialised so that <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softplus}(\rho)\approx 0.09\sqrt{2/d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.09</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softplus}(\rho)\approx 0.09\sqrt{2/d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Training uses KL annealing with the scale frozen at zero for the first epochs, then linearly warmed up.
BatchNormalization layers remain deterministic.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Full-rank layers.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Depthwise convolutions lack the <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix structure required for low-rank factorisation: each channel has an independent <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times k</annotation></semantics></math> kernel (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>), so there is no shared row–column space on which to impose <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=AB^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=AB^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
SE layers are already bottlenecked by the squeeze ratio (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{SE}}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{SE}}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>), yielding weight matrices such as <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="112\times 28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>112</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">112\times 28</annotation></semantics></math> that are smaller than the rank our depth-aware rule would select; further factorisation would over-compress the channel-attention mechanism.
Both cases therefore use direct variational parameterisation with element-wise <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MC Dropout baseline.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also train a standard (deterministic) EfficientNet-B4 with dropout (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>) before the final dense layer.
At test time, dropout is kept active for <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=30</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes, producing MC dropout posterior samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For both models, uncertainty is computed from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=30</annotation></semantics></math> stochastic forward passes, producing <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}\in\Delta^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{p}^{(s)}\in\Delta^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> for each test image.
All metrics in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Complete Metric Reference ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> are computed from these <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Baseline Classification Performance</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ D.3 Baseline Classification Performance ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the Bayesian model’s per-class precision, recall, and F1 on the test set.
The model achieves overall accuracy <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.794" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.794</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.794</annotation></semantics></math> and quadratic weighted kappa <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.646" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.646</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.646</annotation></semantics></math>.
Grade 1 (mild NPDR) is effectively undetectable (F1 <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.00</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with its visual subtlety and small sample size; this does not affect our analysis since Grade 1 belongs to the safe class partition.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Classification report for Bayesian EfficientNet-B4 on DR test set (<math id="A4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7{,}948" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7,948</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7{,}948</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
