<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The confusion matrix reveals the dominant error modes:
Grade 0 absorbs most misclassifications from all other grades (497 from Grade 1, 548 from Grade 2, 107 from Grade 3), reflecting the class imbalance and the model’s conservatism toward the majority class.
Within the critical partition, 220 Grade 3 samples are predicted as Grade 2 (severity underestimate) and 39 Grade 2 samples as Grade 3.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Skewness Diagnostic Analysis</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The skewness diagnostic <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Definition 2.10 (Skewness Diagnostic). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>) assesses whether <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is reliable for each class and sample.
Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ D.4 Skewness Diagnostic Analysis ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports per-class <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> statistics on the full test set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Skewness diagnostic <math id="A4.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by grade (Bayesian model, <math id="A4.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7{,}948" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7,948</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7{,}948</annotation></semantics></math>).
“Reliable” = fraction of samples with <math id="A4.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&lt;0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&lt;0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Grade</th>
<th id="A4.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Median <math id="A4.T7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Mean <math id="A4.T7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T7.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{90}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T7.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Reliable</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">0 (No DR)</th>
<td id="A4.T7.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.012</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.086</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.116</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">94.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T7.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T7.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">1 (Mild)</th>
<td id="A4.T7.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.037</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.082</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.135</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">97.0%</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T7.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T7.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">2 (Moderate)</th>
<td id="A4.T7.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.124</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.198</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.423</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">81.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T7.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T7.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">3 (Severe+PDR)</th>
<td id="A4.T7.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.218</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.328</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.706</td>
<td id="A4.T7.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">63.4%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two patterns emerge.
First, the safe classes (Grades 0–1) have consistently low <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (median <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.04</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;94\%</annotation></semantics></math> reliable), because their high mean probabilities (<math id="A4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\approx 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\approx 0.7</annotation></semantics></math>) place them far from the simplex boundary where skewness is amplified.
Second, the critical classes show progressively higher skewness: Grade 2 (median <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}=0.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}=0.12</annotation></semantics></math>, 82% reliable) and Grade 3 (median <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{3}=0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{3}=0.22</annotation></semantics></math>, 63% reliable), consistent with their low base rates pushing <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> toward the boundary.
Across the full test set, 57% of samples have <em id="A4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> four <math id="A4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&lt;0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&lt;0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Figure <a href="#A4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ D.4 Skewness Diagnostic Analysis ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for the full distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/selective_prediction_skewness.png" id="A4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/87;" width="381" height="87" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Distribution of skewness diagnostic <math id="A4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by class.
Safe classes (Grades 0–1) cluster near zero; critical classes (Grades 2–3) exhibit heavier tails, reflecting boundary suppression effects on rare-class posterior samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These statistics explain the BNN ranking in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Main results. ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Selective Prediction for Diabetic Retinopathy ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: with the majority of critical-class <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates reliable, <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> can exploit the per-class signal effectively.
The MC dropout model (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS11" title="D.11 Full Bootstrap Results: MC Dropout ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.11</span></a>) produces coarser posterior samples (fewer effective degrees of freedom), inflating <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and degrading <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for critical classes, which explains why CBEC, whose correlation gate is Taylor-free and whose restricted domain <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> filters irrelevant co-elevation, outperforms <math id="A4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> in that regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Theory Validation: MI vs. <math id="A4.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ D.5 Theory Validation: MI vs. ∑_𝑘{𝐶_𝑘} ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> plots <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> against exact MI for all 7,948 test samples, complementing the summary statistics reported in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Results ‣ 3 Selective Prediction for Diabetic Retinopathy ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
The slight systematic overestimation visible at high MI values is consistent with the third-order correction in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>: right-skewed distributions near the simplex boundary produce <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{3,k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{3,k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the omitted correction <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}m_{3,k}/\mu_{k}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}m_{3,k}/\mu_{k}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> causes <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to slightly exceed MI.
The right panel confirms this: residuals <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sum_{k}C_{k}-\mathrm{MI})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>MI</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sum_{k}C_{k}-\mathrm{MI})</annotation></semantics></math> are predominantly positive for high-skewness samples (pink/red points), validating that the skewness diagnostic <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> correctly identifies inputs where the approximation degrades.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/selective_prediction_theory_validation.png" id="A4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/109;" width="310" height="109" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><math id="A4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. exact MI for all <math id="A4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}948" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7,948</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}948</annotation></semantics></math> test samples (Pearson <math id="A4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.988" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.988</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.988</annotation></semantics></math>, Spearman <math id="A4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.998" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.998</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.998</annotation></semantics></math>).
<span id="A4.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Scatter plot; the near-perfect rank correlation confirms that the second-order approximation preserves the ordering of epistemic uncertainty.
<span id="A4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Residuals coloured by maximum per-class <math id="A4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; positive residuals concentrate among high-skewness samples, as predicted by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.6 </span>Extended Selective Prediction Analysis</h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.6 Extended Selective Prediction Analysis ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the full selective risk curves for all 10 deferral policies across continuous coverage levels.
The left panel (Critical FNR) confirms that <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the lowest AUSC (0.285), with <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_sum}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_sum</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_sum}}</annotation></semantics></math> second (0.329) and CBEC third (0.415).
The gap widens at lower coverages where clinical operating points typically lie, and the unnormalised variance baselines (Sale_EU_crit: 0.650, Var_crit: 0.606) perform substantially worse than even scalar MI (0.436), confirming that boundary suppression severely degrades raw variance for rare critical classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The right panel addresses a natural concern: does targeting critical-class uncertainty sacrifice overall accuracy?
The answer is no. <math id="A4.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves competitive error-rate AUSC (0.143), comparable to entropy (0.127) and MaxProb (0.126), which optimise for overall accuracy rather than safety.
This demonstrates that the <math id="A4.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposition identifies samples where critical-class confusion drives errors, rather than deferring difficult samples indiscriminately.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A further distinction emerges at the clinically relevant 80% coverage point: <math id="A4.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> retains 1,293 critical samples (27.2% deferred) while achieving FNR <math id="A4.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.302" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.302</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.302</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas MI retains only 1,183 (33.4% deferred) with FNR <math id="A4.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.339" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.339</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.339</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="A4.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is simultaneously <em id="A4.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">less aggressive</em> at deferring critical cases and <em id="A4.SS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more accurate</em> on those it keeps, the hallmark of a well-targeted deferral policy.
Entropy, by contrast, defers 42.1% of critical samples at 80% coverage because it prioritises total uncertainty rather than critical-class epistemic uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/selective_prediction_ausc_curves.png" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/121;" width="357" height="121" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Selective risk curves for all 10 deferral policies.
<span id="A4.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Critical FNR across coverage levels; <math id="A4.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the lowest AUSC (<math id="A4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.285" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.285</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.285</annotation></semantics></math>), dominating all baselines across the full coverage range.
<span id="A4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Error rate; <math id="A4.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains competitive (AUSC <math id="A4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.143" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.143</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.143</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that per-class targeting does not sacrifice overall accuracy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.7 </span>Full Bootstrap Results: Bayesian Model</h3>

<div id="A4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.7 Full Bootstrap Results: Bayesian Model ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> extends Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Main results. ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Selective Prediction for Diabetic Retinopathy ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with all evaluated policies and additional metrics at 80% coverage.
The ranking stability column reports the fraction of 200 bootstrap resamples in which each policy achieves the lowest AUSC.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            We also evaluated a binary cross-boundary MI variant (MI computed on the binary safe-vs-critical partition), which achieves AUSC <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.284\pm 0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.284</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.284\pm 0.015</annotation></semantics></math>, essentially tied with <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{crit\_max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>crit_max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{crit\_max}}</annotation></semantics></math> (mutual win rate 49.5%/50.5%).
It is excluded from the main comparison because it is not part of the <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> framework and exhibits numerical instability (<span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">NaN</span> values when <math id="footnote5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{safe}}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{safe}}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="footnote5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{safe}}\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{safe}}\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T8" class="ltx_table">
