</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The key interpretability result is that errors with similar scalar MI have distinctive <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> fingerprints.
Catastrophic misses (true Grade 3 predicted as Grade 0, <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}107" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>107</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}107</annotation></semantics></math>) and severity underestimates (true Grade 3 predicted as Grade 2, <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>220</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}220</annotation></semantics></math>) have nearly identical scalar MI (<math id="A4.SS9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.024</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.027</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.027</annotation></semantics></math> nats) but strikingly different <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> signatures.
The catastrophic miss concentrates epistemic mass on <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>: the model does not jump directly from severe to healthy, but routes through moderate DR, identifying Grade 2 as the “bottleneck” of confusion.
The severity underestimate instead elevates <math id="A4.SS9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating the model’s primary doubt is whether the image might be healthy.
These qualitatively different failure modes, invisible to any scalar metric, suggest distinct intervention strategies: additional moderate-vs-severe training data for the first, and better healthy-vs-severe discrimination for the second.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.10 </span>Epistemic Confusion Matrix</h3>

<div id="A4.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ D.10 Epistemic Confusion Matrix ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> visualises the epistemic confusion matrix <math id="A4.SS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}=\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}\,\max(0,-\rho_{ij})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}=\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}\,\max(0,-\rho_{ij})]</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies pairwise confusion between classes using the same correlation-gated geometric mean that underlies CBEC (Eq. <a href="#S2.E14" title="In Off-diagonal structure and the CBEC metric. ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ D.10 Epistemic Confusion Matrix ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> provides the numerical values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cross-boundary block (safe<math id="A4.SS10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>critical) dominates: its mean value
is <math id="A4.SS10.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7\times</annotation></semantics></math> the within-safe mean and <math id="A4.SS10.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10.6\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.6\times</annotation></semantics></math> the within-critical
mean, confirming that the model’s epistemic uncertainty concentrates at
the clinically dangerous boundary.
The strongest pairwise confusion is Grade 0<math id="A4.SS10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Grade 2
(<math id="A4.SS10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.08\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.08</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.08\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with the epistemic profiles (Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ D.8 Epistemic Profiles ‣ Appendix D Diabetic Retinopathy Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) showing
Grade 2 as the dominant source of uncertainty for healthy images.
This structure validates both the safe/critical partition and the CBEC
aggregation, which specifically targets cross-boundary confusion.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/diagnostic_confusion_matrix.png" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/120;" width="310" height="120" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Epistemic confusion matrix <math id="A4.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}=\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}\,\max(0,-\rho_{ij})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}=\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{C_{i}C_{j}}\,\max(0,-\rho_{ij})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The cross-boundary block (safe<math id="A4.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>critical, blue outline) dominates, with the Grade 0<math id="A4.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Grade 2 pair (<math id="A4.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.08\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.08</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.08\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>) exhibiting the strongest confusion.
Within-critical confusion is an order of magnitude weaker (<math id="A4.F8.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10.6\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.6\times</annotation></semantics></math> smaller mean), confirming that the model’s epistemic uncertainty concentrates at the clinically dangerous safe/critical boundary.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Epistemic confusion matrix (<math id="A4.T12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, Bayesian model).
Horizontal rule separates the safe (Grades 0–1) and critical (Grades 2–3) partitions; entries in the off-diagonal blocks constitute the cross-boundary confusion aggregated by CBEC.</figcaption>
