</figure>
<div id="A5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For reference, the aggregate <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves an OoD/ID mean-score
ratio of <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="6.43\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.43</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.43\times</annotation></semantics></math> (vs. <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.92\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.92</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.92\times</annotation></semantics></math> for MI and <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.92\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.92</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.92\times</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that the <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalisation in <math id="A5.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
provides a consistent boost over the raw variance sum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Score Distribution Analysis and the <math id="A5.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> Paradox</h3>

<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On MIMIC-III, <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the <em id="A5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">highest</em> mean OoD/ID
ratio (<math id="A5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.705\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.705</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.705\times</annotation></semantics></math>) yet the <em id="A5.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lowest</em> AUROC (<math id="A5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.778" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.778</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.778</annotation></semantics></math>) among the
three primary Bayesian metrics.
Figure <a href="#A5.F12" title='Figure 12 ‣ E.5 Score Distribution Analysis and the 𝐸⁢𝑈_"var" Paradox ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> makes this paradox immediately visual.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/fig_score_distributions_mimic.png" id="A5.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/161;" width="476" height="161" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>MIMIC ICU (blue) vs. Newborn (red) score distributions for the three
primary Bayesian metrics, pooled across 5 seeds
(<math id="A5.F12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\approx}22{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>22,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\approx}22{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> ID samples, <math id="A5.F12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\approx}27{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>27,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\approx}27{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> OoD samples).
Shaded areas: histograms (90 bins, density-normalised).
Solid curves: kernel density estimates (Scott’s rule).
Dashed verticals: distribution means.
<span id="A5.F12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Centre panel:</span> despite achieving the largest mean separation
(<math id="A5.F12.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.705\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.705</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.705\times</annotation></semantics></math>), both ID and OoD <math id="A5.F12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions
concentrate near zero with a shared heavy right tail.
<span id="A5.F12.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right panel:</span> <math id="A5.F12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves better tail separation,
yielding the highest AUROC (<math id="A5.F12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.815" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.815</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.815</annotation></semantics></math>).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why a high mean ratio does not imply better separability.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AUROC measures <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(s_{\mathrm{OoD}}&gt;s_{\mathrm{ID}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>OoD</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ID</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(s_{\mathrm{OoD}}&gt;s_{\mathrm{ID}})</annotation></semantics></math> for a randomly
chosen pair drawn independently from the OoD and ID score distributions.
This probability is governed by the <em id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">full distributional overlap</em>, not
merely the ratio of means.
Under a Gaussian approximation of the log-scores, the relationship is</p>
<table id="A5.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{AUROC}\;\approx\;\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{OoD}}-\mu_{\mathrm{ID}}}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{OoD}}+\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{ID}}}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>AUROC</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>OoD</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ID</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>OoD</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ID</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AUROC}\;\approx\;\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{OoD}}-\mu_{\mathrm{ID}}}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{OoD}}+\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{ID}}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard-normal CDF.
A high OoD/ID mean ratio inflates the numerator, but if the within-group
standard deviations are also large, the denominator grows proportionally
and the AUROC gain is suppressed, analogous to Cohen’s <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why EU<sub id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">var</span></sub> suffers from dynamic range compression.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EU<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\mathrm{var}}=\sum_{k}\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>var</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\mathrm{var}}=\sum_{k}\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> is an un-normalised
variance sum.
For a sigmoid binary classifier, predictive variance is
<em id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heteroscedastic</em>: samples near the decision boundary have high
<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> irrespective of ID/OoD status, simultaneously inflating
the right tail of <em id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> distributions.
Although the OoD mean exceeds the ID mean by a factor of <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.71\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.71</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71\times</annotation></semantics></math>,
the bulk of both distributions concentrates near zero with only a thin
right tail separating them.
The resulting large within-group spread keeps the denominator
of (<a href="#A5.E53" title='In Why a high mean ratio does not imply better separability. ‣ E.5 Score Distribution Analysis and the 𝐸⁢𝑈_"var" Paradox ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>) large, yielding the lowest AUROC
(<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.778" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.778</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.778</annotation></semantics></math>) among the three Bayesian metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves better separation with a lower ratio.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}=\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]\,/\,(2\mu_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}=\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]\,/\,(2\mu_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> normalises each variance component
by the corresponding mean probability.
This acts as a variance-stabilising transform: large absolute variances
that arise when <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is also large are down-weighted, while contributions
from classes with small <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are amplified.
The net effect narrows the within-group spread for both ID and OoD
populations, so a smaller mean ratio (<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.62\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.62</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.62\times</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.71\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.71</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71\times</annotation></semantics></math>)
still translates to cleaner tail separation and a higher AUROC (<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.815" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.815</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.815</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why the paradox is absent on FashionMNIST.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On FashionMNIST<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>KMNIST, <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> and MI attain nearly
identical mean ratios (<math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.923\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.923</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.923\times</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.921\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.921</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.921\times</annotation></semantics></math>), and their
AUROC gap is only <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.014</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.014</annotation></semantics></math>.
The distinction arises because KMNIST induces a strong, directionally
consistent uncertainty lift across all ten softmax outputs, preventing
the range compression that
afflicts <math id="A5.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EU_{\text{var}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU_{\text{var}}</annotation></semantics></math> on MIMIC’s binary sigmoid output.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.6 </span>Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic</h3>

<div id="A5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> establishes that the expected entropy satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p})\bigr]\;\approx\;\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\mu})-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k}\frac{\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]}{\mu_{k}}+\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}\frac{m_{3,k}}{\mu_{k}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p})\bigr]\;\approx\;\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\mu})-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k}\frac{\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]}{\mu_{k}}+\frac{1}{6}\sum_{k}\frac{m_{3,k}}{\mu_{k}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{3,k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k})^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{3,k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(p_{k}^{(s)}-\mu_{k})^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the empirical third central moment.
The reliability of the second-order approximation (i.e. <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>)
is governed by the skewness diagnostic</p>
<table id="A5.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}(x)=\frac{|m_{3,k}|}{3\,\mu_{k}\cdot\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]}=\frac{\bigl|\text{3rd-order correction}\bigr|}{\bigl|\text{2nd-order correction}\bigr|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mtext>3rd-order correction</mtext><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mtext>2nd-order correction</mtext><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}(x)=\frac{|m_{3,k}|}{3\,\mu_{k}\cdot\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]}=\frac{\bigl|\text{3rd-order correction}\bigr|}{\bigl|\text{2nd-order correction}\bigr|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">so <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> signals a reliable quadratic regime and
<math id="A5.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math> signals regime breakdown.
Note that <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\mu_{k}\cdot\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]=6\mu_{k}^{2}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\mu_{k}\cdot\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]=6\mu_{k}^{2}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A5.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}=|m_{3,k}|/(6\mu_{k}^{2}C_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}=|m_{3,k}|/(6\mu_{k}^{2}C_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>: the <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> factor amplifies
<math id="A5.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for classes with small predicted probability, which is precisely
the singularity avoided by using <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main text claims OoD inputs have higher <math id="A5.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> than ID inputs.
Figures <a href="#A5.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ MIMIC-III (𝐾=2). ‣ E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ FashionMNIST (𝐾=10). ‣ E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> provide the first
direct empirical confirmation, with one instructive exception.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MIMIC-III (<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=2</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<figure id="A5.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/fig_rho_skewness_mimic.png" id="A5.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:419/162;" width="419" height="162" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Skewness diagnostic <math id="A5.F13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for ID (ICU, blue) and
OoD (Newborn, red) on MIMIC, pooled across 5 seeds.
Shaded areas: density-normalised histograms clipped to the
99.5th percentile; solid curves: kernel density estimates;
dashed verticals: medians.
<span id="A5.F13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left (<math id="A5.F13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, survive):</span> Both distributions concentrate
well below <math id="A5.F13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that <math id="A5.F13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is reliable for both
populations; the median shift (<math id="A5.F13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.032\to 0.046" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.032</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.046</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.032\to 0.046</annotation></semantics></math>) is statistically
significant but practically small.
<span id="A5.F13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right (<math id="A5.F13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, mortality):</span> The OoD distribution shifts
<em id="A5.F13.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em> (median <math id="A5.F13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.065&lt;0.079" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.065</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.079</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.065&lt;0.079</annotation></semantics></math>), reversing the pattern
predicted by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>; near-zero <math id="A5.F13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for
newborns collapses <math id="A5.F13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> faster than <math id="A5.F13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> compensates,
concentrating the third-order signal in class 0.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.T19" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 19: </span>MIMIC: median and mean of <math id="A5.T19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for ID (ICU) and OoD (Newborn)
populations, pooled across 5 seeds.
Mann–Whitney <math id="A5.T19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> test, two-sided.
</figcaption>
