</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> reversal on MIMIC.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mortality class (<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>) shows the <em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">opposite</em> direction to the
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> prediction: OoD newborns have <em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower</em>
<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> than ICU patients (median <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.065&lt;0.079" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.065</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.079</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.065&lt;0.079</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-130}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>130</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-130}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The mechanism is coherent: the model learns to predict near-zero mortality
probability for newborns, a structurally healthy population.
Consequently, all <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=512</annotation></semantics></math> MC samples pile up near <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
producing a distribution over <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that is <em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tight</em> (small
<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>) and slightly right-skewed, so the third central
moment <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|m_{3,1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|m_{3,1}|</annotation></semantics></math> collapses faster than the <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> amplification in
the denominator of <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can compensate.
The OoD detection signal for MIMIC lives entirely in class 0 (survive):
<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> shifts right by <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the median.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this does not contradict higher epistemic uncertainty for OoD
inputs.
Because <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}=\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]/(2\mu_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}=\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]/(2\mu_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, a smaller <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>
and a simultaneously smaller <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can still yield a larger <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>:
for OoD newborns, <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> collapses faster than <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;C_{1}^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>OoD</mtext></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>ID</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;C_{1}^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#A5.T17" title="Table 17 ‣ Per-class decomposition (MIMIC-III). ‣ E.3 Extended OoD Detection Results ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>)
even while <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]^{\text{OoD}}&lt;\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>OoD</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>ID</mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]^{\text{OoD}}&lt;\mathrm{Var}[p_{1}]^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> normalisation in <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely what decouples epistemic
uncertainty from MC spread, and it is the MC spread, not <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, that
governs <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The reversal is not specific to MIMIC or to minority classes.
The correct sufficient condition is: <em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the MC draws are more
consistent about <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for OoD samples than for ID samples</em>, i.e. <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]^{\text{OoD}}&lt;\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>OoD</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>ID</mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]^{\text{OoD}}&lt;\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> reversal does not invalidate Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>,
which makes no per-class monotonicity claim, but it highlights that
binary-output models can concentrate their third-order regime into a single
class depending on the OoD population’s characteristics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FashionMNIST (<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<figure id="A5.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/fig_rho_skewness_fmnist.png" id="A5.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Per-class skewness diagnostic <math id="A5.F14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for FashionMNIST (ID, blue)
vs. KMNIST (OoD, orange), averaged over 5 seeds.
Error bars: seed standard deviation.
All ten categories show <math id="A5.F14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;\rho_{k}^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>OoD</mtext></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>ID</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;\rho_{k}^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
confirming that KMNIST inputs produce systematically more asymmetric
MC posteriors across every class, with OoD/ID ratios ranging from
<math id="A5.F14.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.4\times</annotation></semantics></math> (Ankle boot) to <math id="A5.F14.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="14.2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.2\times</annotation></semantics></math> (Sandal).
The directional pattern is consistent with the <math id="A5.F14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> profile
(Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ E.4 Per-Class Epistemic Decomposition for FashionMNIST ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), though the per-class rankings differ,
as <math id="A5.F14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on third-moment structure rather than variance alone.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.T20" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 20: </span>FashionMNIST: per-class mean <math id="A5.T20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for ID and OoD populations,
averaged over 5 seeds, with Mann–Whitney <math id="A5.T20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A5.T20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values computed on
pooled samples (<math id="A5.T20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> per split per class).
</figcaption>
