<section id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These figures close the loop between Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9 (Third-Order Correction). ‣ 2.5 Reliability Diagnostic via Skewness ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> and
the empirical OoD results.
OoD inputs are not merely <em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more uncertain</em> (higher <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) but more
<em id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymmetrically uncertain</em> (higher <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>), at least for classes
where the OoD population has meaningful predicted probability mass.
When <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> (ID regime), the second-order approximation is
trustworthy and <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}\approx\text{MI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mtext>MI</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}\approx\text{MI}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the two metrics converge.
When <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is large (OoD regime), the cubic correction
in (<a href="#A5.E54" title="In E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) becomes significant; <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> captures this
additional signal through its <math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> weighting, while MI averages
over the sampling distribution without per-class amplification.
On FashionMNIST, where KMNIST induces consistently higher uncertainty
across all ten logit outputs, the reversal is absent: every class shows
<math id="A5.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;\rho_{k}^{\text{ID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>OoD</mtext></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>ID</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}^{\text{OoD}}&gt;\rho_{k}^{\text{ID}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#A5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ FashionMNIST (𝐾=10). ‣ E.6 Empirical Verification of the Skewness Diagnostic ‣ Appendix E OoD Detection Experiment: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.7 </span>Deep Ensemble OoD Detection: MIMIC-III</h3>

<div id="A5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We repeat the MIMIC-III ICU<math id="A5.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>Newborn OoD detection experiment using
a deep ensemble of five members (one deterministic forward pass per
member) in place of the low-rank Bayesian model.
The ensemble uses the same 44-feature pipeline and identical evaluation
code; uncertainty is derived from disagreement across members rather
than MC sampling.
As the ensemble consists of a single trained instance, no seed standard
deviation is reported.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T21" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 21: </span>OoD detection on MIMIC-III ICU<math id="A5.T21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>Newborn: deep ensemble.
AUROC and OoD/ID mean-score ratio. Best in <span id="A5.T21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
