<figure id="A6.F17" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21160v1/figures/experiment3b_all_models_heatmap.png" id="A6.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:176/275;" width="176" height="275" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 17: </span>Mean <math id="A6.F17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|</annotation></semantics></math> averaged over <math id="A6.F17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.1,\dots,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.1,\dots,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all ten model/dataset combinations.
Green indicates near-zero leakage; red indicates high entanglement.
End-to-end low-rank models (top rows) achieve the strongest disentanglement for both metrics.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Key patterns across transfer-learning experiments.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The controlled comparison on CIFAR-10 is decisive: using the <em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same dataset</em> (<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}10</annotation></semantics></math>) and <em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same model type</em> (low-rank), training from scratch yields <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> while transfer learning yields <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.74" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.74</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.74</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.97</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.97</annotation></semantics></math>.
This directly isolates the training regime as a causal factor.
Within transfer learning, higher baseline inflation (<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}\,/\,\text{MI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">/</mo><mtext>MI</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}\,/\,\text{MI}</annotation></semantics></math>) correlates with worse <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> performance relative to MI, a pattern that holds for both CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100.
The implication is that when the entire model participates in the Bayesian posterior, the resulting predictive distributions have a variance structure that is well captured by the <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> normalisation; when only a classifier head is Bayesian, this structure degrades.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of rank constraint on uncertainty scale.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The low-rank variational framework of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toure and Stephens [2026]</a></cite>
parameterizes each weight matrix as <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=AB^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=AB^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with independent
mean-field Gaussian posteriors <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(A)q(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(A)q(B)</annotation></semantics></math> on the factors
<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The induced posterior on <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">singular</em> with respect to
Lebesgue measure, concentrating entirely on the manifold
<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank-<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> matrices: every sampled weight matrix is
constrained to have rank at most <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, though the specific column and
row spaces vary across posterior samples.
Under the same transfer-learning protocol on CIFAR-10, the low-rank
model (<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>) produces baseline MI an order of magnitude below its
full-rank counterpart (<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0045</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0045</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.039" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.039</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.039</annotation></semantics></math>), a gap that persists
across all noise levels (e.g., at <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.5</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.047" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.047</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.047</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.072</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.072</annotation></semantics></math>).
The from-scratch low-rank model exhibits a similar absolute scale
(<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MI}=0.0067" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>MI</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0067</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MI}=0.0067</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that the compression is driven
primarily by the rank constraint rather than the training regime.
This compression has two distinct sources.
First, the rank constraint itself restricts the number of
independent directions along which weight matrices can vary; the
resulting predictive distributions inherit this reduced
dimensionality, yielding smaller <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[p_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> and thus smaller MI.
Second, while the factored posterior introduces structured
correlations between weight entries (<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(W_{ij},W_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(W_{ij},W_{i^{\prime}j^{\prime}})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
whenever the entries share latent factors), it remains mean-field
<em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within</em> each factor, which is known to underestimate marginal
variances relative to the true
posterior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blei et al., 2017</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner and Sahani, 2011</a>]</cite>, further
concentrating the predictive distribution.
Crucially, this compression affects the <em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absolute scale</em> of
uncertainty but not the <em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative ranking</em> of inputs or the
<em id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-model metric comparison</em>: since <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and MI
share the same compressed scale by construction
(Equation <a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty Vector ‣ 2 Per-Class Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), their relative disentanglement ratios
<math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{rel}}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{rel}}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> remain directly comparable within a given model, and the
finding that <math id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> leaks less than MI holds independently of
the overall scale.
However, uncertainty thresholds calibrated on one posterior family
cannot be transferred to another without recalibration, and the small
absolute values produced by low-rank models should not be interpreted
as evidence of low true epistemic uncertainty but rather as a
consequence of both the manifold constraint on the posterior support
and the mean-field under-dispersion within the learned factors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Sensitivity to Class Cardinality</h3>

<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> normalisation that corrects boundary suppression also introduces a scaling effect with the number of classes <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under the simplex constraint <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}\bar{p}_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}\bar{p}_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> grows the average probability per class decreases.
For a model assigning probability <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to the predicted class and distributing the remainder uniformly:</p>
<table id="A6.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{k}\;=\;\frac{1-\alpha}{K-1}\quad\text{for }k\neq\text{pred}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mtext>pred</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{k}\;=\;\frac{1-\alpha}{K-1}\quad\text{for }k\neq\text{pred}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Assuming approximately uniform variance <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(p_{k})\approx\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(p_{k})\approx\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> across classes:</p>
<table id="A6.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}\;\approx\;\frac{\sigma^{2}}{2\alpha}\;+\;\frac{(K-1)^{2}\,\sigma^{2}}{2(1-\alpha)}\;\sim\;\mathcal{O}(K^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}\;\approx\;\frac{\sigma^{2}}{2\alpha}\;+\;\frac{(K-1)^{2}\,\sigma^{2}}{2(1-\alpha)}\;\sim\;\mathcal{O}(K^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">whereas MI, measuring entropy differences bounded by <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log K</annotation></semantics></math>, does not accumulate linearly over classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, this manifests as higher baseline inflation on CIFAR-100 compared to CIFAR-10 for every model type (Table <a href="#A6.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ Baseline inflation. ‣ F.2 Transfer Learning Experiments ‣ Appendix F Epistemic Sensitivity to Data Quality: Supplementary Details ‣ Not Just How Much, But Where: Decomposing Epistemic Uncertainty into Per-Class Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>): low-rank <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.17\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.17</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.17\times</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.08\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.08</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.08\times</annotation></semantics></math>; full-rank <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.59\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.59</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.59\times</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.35\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.35</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.35\times</annotation></semantics></math>; MC dropout <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.89\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.89</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.89\times</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.62\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.62</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.62\times</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dependence interacts with model type: low-rank models remain resilient at <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}100</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A6.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|&lt;0.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|&lt;0.37</annotation></semantics></math>), while MC dropout fails substantially (<math id="A6.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{\mathrm{rel}}|</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.78</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mitigation strategies.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For high-cardinality settings (<math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\gtrsim 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≳</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\gtrsim 50</annotation></semantics></math>), two modifications can reduce inflation without sacrificing the per-class interpretability of <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>:
(i) <em id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">truncated summation</em>, restricting <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the top-<math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most probable classes, which eliminates the accumulation of amplified terms from negligible-probability classes;
(ii) <em id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probability-weighted aggregation</em>, weighting each <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to down-weight low-probability contributions.
We recommend reporting both <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and MI in high-<math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> settings: the per-class decomposition retains interpretive value even when the aggregate <math id="A6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> underperforms MI as a scalar summary.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
